WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.135
&gt;&gt; So, as we

00:00:06.136 --> 00:00:08.715
heard a talk from Bempu
Healthcare yesterday,

00:00:08.716 --> 00:00:12.690
their team has this baby
incubator, like a setup,

00:00:12.691 --> 00:00:15.790
which helps babies
who are either born

00:00:15.791 --> 00:00:17.695
prematurely or who have

00:00:17.696 --> 00:00:19.835
been in the ICU
for a day or more.

00:00:19.836 --> 00:00:21.245
When they're brought back home,

00:00:21.246 --> 00:00:22.990
how can we also give
them a level of

00:00:22.991 --> 00:00:25.110
care especially in rural areas?

00:00:25.111 --> 00:00:26.665
So, they've developed
this system which can

00:00:26.666 --> 00:00:28.790
have a smartphone
tracking the baby,

00:00:28.791 --> 00:00:32.380
and they also ask questions
regularly through nurses.

00:00:32.381 --> 00:00:34.345
So, we thought of how we can use

00:00:34.346 --> 00:00:36.500
AI for such a system and this is,

00:00:36.501 --> 00:00:38.730
I'm speaking on behalf
of my team, of course,

00:00:38.731 --> 00:00:40.765
people from Bempu
Healthcare represented,

00:00:40.766 --> 00:00:43.295
from MSR India we have Jackie,

00:00:43.296 --> 00:00:45.900
USC Brian was s team member,

00:00:45.901 --> 00:00:47.285
and from Triple IT Delhi,

00:00:47.286 --> 00:00:50.040
Angipan Golal, are in the team.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:54.185
So, our societal problem
is to prevent

00:00:54.186 --> 00:00:58.700
neonatal deaths and not all
babies can be kept in ICUs,

00:00:58.701 --> 00:01:00.560
so you have to think of what
we can do when they're at

00:01:00.561 --> 00:01:02.990
home just when
they're discharged.

00:01:02.991 --> 00:01:04.845
These are high-risk babies,

00:01:04.846 --> 00:01:06.595
low birth weight, premature.

00:01:06.596 --> 00:01:08.420
The current climate is that there

00:01:08.421 --> 00:01:10.410
are two solutions available.

00:01:10.411 --> 00:01:12.020
They're either ASHA workers

00:01:12.021 --> 00:01:13.670
who are supposed to
make home visits,

00:01:13.671 --> 00:01:14.870
but it's hard for them to keep

00:01:14.871 --> 00:01:17.295
track and they cannot
visit as often.

00:01:17.296 --> 00:01:19.430
The second solution
is what Bempu has

00:01:19.431 --> 00:01:21.840
proposed which is more like
a sensor- based system.

00:01:21.841 --> 00:01:25.160
What we would like to do
is to see how they can

00:01:25.161 --> 00:01:27.200
deploy this proposal and

00:01:27.201 --> 00:01:29.325
the system to many more patients.

00:01:29.326 --> 00:01:31.130
They tell us the problem they

00:01:31.131 --> 00:01:32.870
have is that it's not
the government that they

00:01:32.871 --> 00:01:34.430
actually have approval
to deploy this

00:01:34.431 --> 00:01:36.620
to many more children,

00:01:36.621 --> 00:01:37.760
but the problem is
they don't have

00:01:37.761 --> 00:01:39.350
the resources because theirs is

00:01:39.351 --> 00:01:42.690
a very resource intensive model.

00:01:42.691 --> 00:01:46.664
So, they have doctors who
look at these videos,

00:01:46.665 --> 00:01:49.685
and they also have
nurses who call twice

00:01:49.686 --> 00:01:53.315
every day each of these
parents of babies.

00:01:53.316 --> 00:01:55.485
So, we thought one way

00:01:55.486 --> 00:01:58.160
AI or machine learning
could help is in

00:01:58.161 --> 00:02:00.605
simply predicting risk of

00:02:00.606 --> 00:02:03.620
different babies as we
get data from them.

00:02:03.621 --> 00:02:07.680
I'll talk about the exact kinds
of data we have.

00:02:07.681 --> 00:02:10.430
The second problem we
can also think of is

00:02:10.431 --> 00:02:13.000
that it might help in two ways.

00:02:13.001 --> 00:02:15.170
One is it can reduce
the manual effort.

00:02:15.171 --> 00:02:17.720
So for example, if a nurse has

00:02:17.721 --> 00:02:20.810
10 or 20 babies to keep
track of every day,

00:02:20.811 --> 00:02:23.630
the nurse may prioritize
who to call and who to

00:02:23.631 --> 00:02:26.490
ask more questions and spend
more time with as a triage,

00:02:26.491 --> 00:02:29.470
and the second is
it can also help

00:02:29.471 --> 00:02:32.030
Bempu Healthcare scale
up their system to

00:02:32.031 --> 00:02:33.380
more people and also

00:02:33.381 --> 00:02:35.110
look at which signals
are really important.

00:02:35.111 --> 00:02:36.755
So, maybe it is that

00:02:36.756 --> 00:02:38.630
some parts of the video
are not important,

00:02:38.631 --> 00:02:40.490
or maybe some questions
are not important.

00:02:40.491 --> 00:02:41.650
So based on this analysis,

00:02:41.651 --> 00:02:43.160
they will be able to fine-tune

00:02:43.161 --> 00:02:45.485
their system for a low cost.

00:02:45.486 --> 00:02:47.900
So, the concrete problem we have

00:02:47.901 --> 00:02:50.330
identified is that we would

00:02:50.331 --> 00:02:51.920
like to look at all the signals

00:02:51.921 --> 00:02:54.975
that we get currently
from Bempu system,

00:02:54.976 --> 00:02:56.690
and just model for now as

00:02:56.691 --> 00:02:58.515
a simple classification problem,

00:02:58.516 --> 00:03:01.675
is the baby at its
current state healthy,

00:03:01.676 --> 00:03:05.615
or are they showing
any signs of abnormality,

00:03:05.616 --> 00:03:08.970
or extremely, they need some
medical attention right now.

00:03:08.971 --> 00:03:10.815
So, these are the three
categories Bempu Healthcare

00:03:10.816 --> 00:03:14.390
already has labeled
in their data.

00:03:14.391 --> 00:03:16.870
So in our case,

00:03:16.871 --> 00:03:19.720
we have already some data
available which

00:03:19.721 --> 00:03:22.955
their team is happy to
share after this workshop.

00:03:22.956 --> 00:03:25.460
The data consists of 100 patients

00:03:25.461 --> 00:03:28.415
on which they have
the system deployed.

00:03:28.416 --> 00:03:30.030
They found that out of them,

00:03:30.031 --> 00:03:32.775
four were readmitted so they
were really extreme cases,

00:03:32.776 --> 00:03:34.580
and 20 required follow-up,

00:03:34.581 --> 00:03:35.630
and because they were doing it in

00:03:35.631 --> 00:03:37.250
a very manually intensive way,

00:03:37.251 --> 00:03:38.480
they were able to track these

00:03:38.481 --> 00:03:41.500
patients and do the follow-up.

00:03:41.501 --> 00:03:44.840
So, what we are thinking
of is if we have the data

00:03:44.841 --> 00:03:48.225
which is demographic and
medical history of the baby,

00:03:48.226 --> 00:03:50.600
we have the vital signs data
for some patients,

00:03:50.601 --> 00:03:52.700
not all because they
have these sensors,

00:03:52.701 --> 00:03:55.475
we have videos, four per day,

00:03:55.476 --> 00:03:57.260
which means like four
times the camera

00:03:57.261 --> 00:03:58.970
activates and records the video,

00:03:58.971 --> 00:04:00.650
and then we have
two phone calls from

00:04:00.651 --> 00:04:03.730
nurses which will also
happens twice per day.

00:04:03.731 --> 00:04:05.390
So, even though it might

00:04:05.391 --> 00:04:06.780
look that we only
have 100 patients,

00:04:06.781 --> 00:04:08.490
if you look at
the actual sample size,

00:04:08.491 --> 00:04:10.130
these four videos per day for

00:04:10.131 --> 00:04:12.305
each baby they have two weeks.

00:04:12.306 --> 00:04:16.980
So it's about 70 datapoints
approximately per baby.

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:21.150
So proposed solution
will go in two parts.

00:04:21.151 --> 00:04:23.630
One is that we're going to
do an exploratory analysis

00:04:23.631 --> 00:04:24.950
first just to see

00:04:24.951 --> 00:04:27.409
which kinds of signals
are more predictive,

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:30.590
are correlated with
the bad outcomes,

00:04:30.591 --> 00:04:33.250
either an abnormality
or a needs attention.

00:04:33.251 --> 00:04:35.390
The second task would be
based on these insights,

00:04:35.391 --> 00:04:37.910
you want to build
a classifier to predict

00:04:37.911 --> 00:04:40.700
whether a baby is healthy or not.

00:04:40.701 --> 00:04:43.220
The idea is that if, and you
want to be conservative,

00:04:43.221 --> 00:04:45.790
so you only want to say
a baby's healthy when

00:04:45.791 --> 00:04:48.620
it's on the far end
of really healthy.

00:04:48.621 --> 00:04:50.465
If you find any sign
of abnormality,

00:04:50.466 --> 00:04:53.000
we actually want to
raise it and then

00:04:53.001 --> 00:04:56.225
we'll have a nurse or
a doctor take it from there.

00:04:56.226 --> 00:05:00.500
Finally, of course, it also
can help Bempu later on to

00:05:00.501 --> 00:05:02.080
fine-tune their product
to only collect

00:05:02.081 --> 00:05:04.740
a certain set of
data and not all.

00:05:04.741 --> 00:05:05.795
These are some examples of

00:05:05.796 --> 00:05:08.175
images that they have collected,

00:05:08.176 --> 00:05:10.100
and we would like
to, essentially,

00:05:10.101 --> 00:05:13.100
let's say if this baby is
healthier than this baby,

00:05:13.101 --> 00:05:17.020
then you want to check that
and be able to use video.

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:20.445
So, the estimated impact.

00:05:20.446 --> 00:05:22.460
This is just a
calculation that we

00:05:22.461 --> 00:05:24.805
did among our team members.

00:05:24.806 --> 00:05:27.500
We have found that four babies
out of the 100 that

00:05:27.501 --> 00:05:30.290
hadn't trial required
readmission.

00:05:30.291 --> 00:05:33.590
So what it means
is that as we get

00:05:33.591 --> 00:05:36.700
more and more babies
in the systems,

00:05:36.701 --> 00:05:39.140
then nurses could prioritize,

00:05:39.141 --> 00:05:41.840
and therefore get
more people having

00:05:41.841 --> 00:05:45.640
a chance to be treated and
readmitted to hospitals.

00:05:45.641 --> 00:05:48.770
It will also help us to think
of how we can also enable

00:05:48.771 --> 00:05:51.980
the ASHA workers who are
not a part of this system,

00:05:51.981 --> 00:05:53.855
but maybe that
learnings that we have

00:05:53.856 --> 00:05:56.120
of either phone calls
or some kinds of

00:05:56.121 --> 00:05:58.370
sensors could also be used

00:05:58.371 --> 00:06:01.810
when ASHA workers
do their visits.

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:05.640
Ethical concerns.
I think there are

00:06:05.641 --> 00:06:08.915
two ethical concerns
that we discussed.

00:06:08.916 --> 00:06:12.980
So, the first is if

00:06:12.981 --> 00:06:17.420
our detector is raising a lot
of alarms for no reason,

00:06:17.421 --> 00:06:20.355
parents might get worried and
get anxious for no reason.

00:06:20.356 --> 00:06:22.415
The other more
serious one is that

00:06:22.416 --> 00:06:24.455
if our detector makes mistakes,

00:06:24.456 --> 00:06:26.650
parents might feel everything is

00:06:26.651 --> 00:06:28.220
fine whereas their baby

00:06:28.221 --> 00:06:30.035
might be having
a medical problem.

00:06:30.036 --> 00:06:32.375
Our current solution with this

00:06:32.376 --> 00:06:34.440
is to be as conservative
as possible.

00:06:34.441 --> 00:06:39.575
So always, it's better to
raise false alarms than not,

00:06:39.576 --> 00:06:41.195
in our case at least, we think,

00:06:41.196 --> 00:06:43.535
but this is something we
have to talk to doctors

00:06:43.536 --> 00:06:46.765
and Bempu team to figure
this straight off.

00:06:46.766 --> 00:06:49.305
The other two are, I think,

00:06:49.306 --> 00:06:50.990
a common concern with using data,

00:06:50.991 --> 00:06:53.730
so we would want to protect
patient information.

00:06:53.731 --> 00:06:56.090
So I think, we'll
try to see if we can

00:06:56.091 --> 00:06:58.990
anonymize all the data and
especially the videos,

00:06:58.991 --> 00:07:01.340
and also how patient
consent which Bempus I

00:07:01.341 --> 00:07:04.345
think already has for doing
research with this data.

00:07:04.346 --> 00:07:07.150
One concern, although it's
not an ethical concern,

00:07:07.151 --> 00:07:09.210
I think it's more
of a data concern,

00:07:09.211 --> 00:07:12.715
is that not all of
the parents comply,

00:07:12.716 --> 00:07:17.105
so we won't have data
for all of these babies

00:07:17.106 --> 00:07:18.770
on all times but I
think that's also

00:07:18.771 --> 00:07:21.890
an interesting technical
challenge to look at.

00:07:21.891 --> 00:07:23.575
So our concrete next steps.

00:07:23.576 --> 00:07:26.450
Luckily, we are in a position
where we can access

00:07:26.451 --> 00:07:28.330
the data and the Bempu team

00:07:28.331 --> 00:07:30.145
has kindly accepted to share it.

00:07:30.146 --> 00:07:31.855
Our plan is in
the next two months,

00:07:31.856 --> 00:07:34.540
we'll do exploratory
analysis to find

00:07:34.541 --> 00:07:36.640
the key signals that really

00:07:36.641 --> 00:07:39.190
matter in terms of
the outcomes we have,

00:07:39.191 --> 00:07:40.330
and then as a part of

00:07:40.331 --> 00:07:43.180
that same process in
the next four to six months,

00:07:43.181 --> 00:07:47.620
we would want to deploy
a classifier that can just

00:07:47.621 --> 00:07:48.880
look at these sensors and help

00:07:48.881 --> 00:07:52.195
nurses make
prioritized decisions.

00:07:52.196 --> 00:07:53.890
Beyond that, I think our goal is

00:07:53.891 --> 00:07:55.300
that it actually gets
integrated into what

00:07:55.301 --> 00:07:58.885
Bempu is doing and we do
trials with this technology.

00:07:58.886 --> 00:08:01.600
That's it. Thank you.

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:08.535
&gt;&gt; The baby classification,

00:08:08.536 --> 00:08:12.730
is this for the baby or
the whole time or is it,

00:08:12.731 --> 00:08:14.090
at this point in time

00:08:14.091 --> 00:08:16.055
this baby does not look
healthy or healthy,

00:08:16.056 --> 00:08:19.200
so it's sort of much more
period by time period.

00:08:19.201 --> 00:08:21.055
&gt;&gt; Yes, it is the time period

00:08:21.056 --> 00:08:22.660
by time period classification.

00:08:22.661 --> 00:08:24.620
In fact, I think what
we'll actually do is

00:08:24.621 --> 00:08:26.995
to look at only the change
in each baby.

00:08:26.996 --> 00:08:29.665
So, at any point of
time, let's say time t,

00:08:29.666 --> 00:08:31.350
we look at the baby's signals and

00:08:31.351 --> 00:08:33.800
video at time t minus
1 and we try to see

00:08:33.801 --> 00:08:35.345
how much this change

00:08:35.346 --> 00:08:38.370
corresponds to a healthy
versus a abnormality.

00:08:38.371 --> 00:08:40.090
But really, I think
we want to do it at

00:08:40.091 --> 00:08:45.375
the same time so that
nurses can respond.

00:08:45.376 --> 00:08:49.750
&gt;&gt; Very quick question while
the next group sets up.

00:08:49.790 --> 00:09:01.860
&gt;&gt; If you have many the cases where
the babies actually [inaudible]

00:09:01.861 --> 00:09:04.575
&gt;&gt; Yes. I think
that's a good point.

00:09:04.576 --> 00:09:07.310
So, as I said, we have to
strike the right balance

00:09:07.311 --> 00:09:10.400
but I think the other side
is way more worse.

00:09:10.401 --> 00:09:12.360
So we took the conservative side.

00:09:12.361 --> 00:09:13.910
But hopefully, I think
our algorithms will

00:09:13.911 --> 00:09:15.200
be able to detect with

00:09:15.201 --> 00:09:18.790
some accuracy that this
trade-off is little reduced.

